{
	title: 'One-command Hal server setup over SSH',
	spec: 'scripts/server-setup <ssh-host> [public-name] sets up or updates Hal on a Linux server that SSH reaches without prompts, non-interactively and in the foreground so its progress shows live. The public name defaults to the SSH host without user@. Without user@ it tries the SSH default, root and common cloud image logins (ec2-user, ubuntu, admin, debian, centos, rocky, almalinux, fedora, opc, azureuser) until one works; a failure other than a refused login (or a root stub saying Please login as) stops the search. It runs as that user: root acts directly, others use sudo -n, and anything that cannot get root stops with the reason. Supported: Debian, Ubuntu, RHEL-compatible (Rocky, Alma, CentOS Stream), Fedora and other apt/dnf/zypper/pacman systems, with an official Caddy binary as fallback. scripts/server-pack writes the payload: a Git bundle of the committed local branch (uncommitted changes stay local and are named), the local origin URL, USER.md, config.ason and plugins exporting portable = true that plugins/.syncignore does not list. The remote side clones or fast-forwards ~/hal from the bundle without needing access to origin, sets origin to the local origin URL, installs prerequisites and Bun (./install -y), copies the user files, sets webUrl to https://<public-name> and webPort to the running service port or the first free port from 9001, installs the systemd user unit (scripts/install-service) with linger, and (re)starts it when code, unit or port changed. Before changing the web server it checks that the public name resolves to the server. It refuses to change a server whose ports 80/443 belong to something other than Caddy; otherwise it installs Caddy if missing and adds a site block in its own imported file, never replacing the Caddyfile, validating before reload. The site proxies to the loopback port and answers 502/503/504 with a page saying Hal is not answering and how to inspect it. It opens 80/443 in an active ufw or firewalld. It ends only after https://<public-name> answers as Hal from the local machine, then prints the URL and three one-time login codes, plus a hint to connect with hal -r and ask the remote Hal to set up push access when the local checkout can push to origin but the server cannot; the script itself installs and configures no Git credentials (the user may pull only, use another Git host or none). The skill reply says provider logins are not copied: OAuth tokens shared between hosts break refresh. Rerunning is safe. skills/hal-server-install/SKILL.md is small and self-contained: run the script, ask the user about failures, discuss alternatives to Caddy + systemd before scripting them, and finish with a fixed message using the codes. Tracked files name only reserved example domains.',
	status: 'planned',
	notes: [
		'Checked locally: server-pack output, cloning the bundle, config.update setting webPort/webUrl with comments kept, failure messages, and the generated Caddy site (caddy validate on 2.11; a dead backend gives the 502 page). Not yet run against a real server.'
	],
	needs: ['1es']
}
